<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!-- The cold itself.

       Its own damage def rather than vanilla Frostbite, for two reasons. Frostbite's worker is
       DamageWorker_Frostbite, which is written for a pawn slowly losing extremities to weather
       and is not externalViolence - a raider frozen by a colonist would register as nobody's
       fault. And its heat energy is the wrong sign: an explosion of cold that warms the room is
       a bad joke, so this one cools the cells it touches instead.

       The damage is small on purpose. A frost bomb that kills is a frag grenade with a blue
       texture, and the whole point of the item is the seconds it buys rather than the bodies.

       The freeze hangs off this def rather than off the projectile that delivers it. Anything
       that explodes as AG_Cryo freezes what it touches - a thrown bomb, or a rack of them
       cooking off in a fire - and, more to the point, the grenade can then be whatever class the
       loaded mods want it to be. See DamageWorker_Cryo. -->
  <DamageDef>
    <defName>AG_Cryo</defName>
    <label>cryogenic burst</label>
    <workerClass>RimArt.DamageWorker_Cryo</workerClass>
    <externalViolence>true</externalViolence>
    <isExplosive>true</isExplosive>
    <deathMessage>{0} has been frozen to death.</deathMessage>
    <hediff>Frostbite</hediff>
    <harmAllLayersUntilOutside>true</harmAllLayersUntilOutside>
    <makesBlood>false</makesBlood>
    <canUseDeflectMetalEffect>false</canUseDeflectMetalEffect>
    <!-- Heat armour is what insulates, so heat armour is what resists this. -->
    <armorCategory>Heat</armorCategory>
    <defaultDamage>5</defaultDamage>
    <defaultArmorPenetration>0.10</defaultArmorPenetration>
    <buildingDamageFactorImpassable>0.1</buildingDamageFactorImpassable>
    <buildingDamageFactorPassable>0.1</buildingDamageFactorPassable>
    <plantDamageFactor>1</plantDamageFactor>
    <explosionHeatEnergyPerCell>-16</explosionHeatEnergyPerCell>
    <explosionSnowMeltAmount>0</explosionSnowMeltAmount>
    <explosionCellFleck>BlastDry</explosionCellFleck>
    <explosionColorCenter>(0.80, 0.94, 1.00)</explosionColorCenter>
    <explosionColorEdge>(0.45, 0.70, 0.88)</explosionColorEdge>
    <soundExplosion>Explosion_Stun</soundExplosion>
  </DamageDef>

  <!-- The grenade in flight.

       Parented to the game's own BaseGrenadeProjectile so it arcs, casts a shadow and is
       recognised as a grenade by everything that cares what a grenade is. A zero explosion delay
       makes the bomb detonate on impact.

       thingClass is stated rather than inherited, and that line is load-bearing under Combat
       Extended: their patch rewrites BaseGrenadeProjectile's thingClass to ProjectileCE_Explosive,
       and a child that names its own class keeps it. Verb_ThrowFrostBomb spawns a Verse.Projectile,
       and CE's projectile is not one - so the two have to agree, and this is where they do. -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseGrenadeProjectile">
    <defName>AG_FrostBombProjectile</defName>
    <label>frost bomb</label>
    <thingClass>Projectile_Explosive</thingClass>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>RimArt/Frost/Bomb</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>0.75</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <projectile>
      <explosionRadius>2.9</explosionRadius>
      <damageDef>AG_Cryo</damageDef>
      <explosionDelay>0</explosionDelay>
      <speed>13</speed>
    </projectile>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The bomb, as a thing a pawn carries and throws.

       A weapon, not an ability. It was an ability granted by a worn bandolier, which meant a
       grenade that appeared in the ability bar with a cooldown ring on it and behaved like a
       psycast - so it is equipped, aimed and thrown like the game's own grenades instead. The
       bandolier is gone; this is the item, and the only thing that grants it is crafting one.

       Reusable rather than consumed, exactly like every grenade in the base game: the tubes hold
       charge and the compressor builds the next bomb between throws, which is what the cooldown
       below is. That stays true with Combat Extended loaded, where hand grenades are normally
       one-use ammo - see Patch_CombatExtended for why this one is not.

       No AI weapon tags. Nothing in this mod is handed to raiders, and a stun this cheap in the
       hands of a siege is not a fight, so AG_FrostBomb appears in no pawn kind's gear. -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMakeableGrenade">
    <defName>AG_FrostBomb</defName>
    <label>frost bomb</label>
    <description>A cryogenic charge in a throwable tube. It bursts into a small freezing cloud: everything caught in it stops moving and cannot fight for a few seconds, then thaws back to normal on its own.\n\nIt does not choose sides. Your own people freeze in it exactly as well as the people you threw it at, and frozen targets can still be shot. The compressor rebuilds the charge between throws, so the tube is never used up.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>RimArt/Frost/Bomb</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
    </graphicData>
    <uiIconPath>RimArt/Frost/IconFrost</uiIconPath>
    <uiIconScale>1.1</uiIconScale>
    <soundInteract>Interact_Grenade</soundInteract>
    <techLevel>Industrial</techLevel>
    <!-- Required by the CompProperties_Explosive below, which refuses any other ticker: a comp
         that counts down a wick cannot be ticked rarely. The game says so at load - "CompExplosive
         requires Normal ticker type" - and the vanilla grenades carry the same line. -->
    <tickerType>Normal</tickerType>
    <statBases>
      <Mass>1</Mass>
      <!-- Six seconds between throws, against 2.66 for a frag grenade.

           Two things set this floor and they agree. The freeze is a hard stun, and an
           infinitely reusable stun on a short timer is a chain-lock rather than a tool - the
           item is meant to buy seconds, not to remove somebody from the fight. And the throw
           animation is 72 ticks long with the hand opening at tick 35, so a cooldown shorter
           than 1.2s would let a pawn walk out of the stance while the arm is still coming
           round. Six is comfortably past both. -->
      <RangedWeapon_Cooldown>6</RangedWeapon_Cooldown>
      <WorkToMake>9000</WorkToMake>
      <Flammability>0.6</Flammability>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Steel>20</Steel>
      <ComponentIndustrial>2</ComponentIndustrial>
      <Chemfuel>40</Chemfuel>
    </costList>
    <recipeMaker>
      <researchPrerequisite>AG_CryogenicMunitions</researchPrerequisite>
      <skillRequirements>
        <Crafting>5</Crafting>
      </skillRequirements>
      <displayPriority>320</displayPriority>
    </recipeMaker>
    <thingCategories>
      <li>Grenades</li>
    </thingCategories>
    <tradeTags>
      <li>ExoticMisc</li>
    </tradeTags>
    <smeltable>true</smeltable>
    <verbs>
      <li>
        <label>throw frost bomb</label>
        <verbClass>RimArt.Verb_ThrowFrostBomb</verbClass>
        <hasStandardCommand>true</hasStandardCommand>
        <!-- Matches the game's own grenades. The animation shows a person throwing something by
             hand, and a hand-thrown object that outranges a frag grenade would be a lie told
             twice. -->
        <range>12.9</range>
        <!-- Pinpoint, deliberately, and 0.4 rather than 0 because the two numbers are read by
             two different pieces of the game.

             ThingDef error checking requires a forced miss radius on a verb that launches an
             explosive projectile and refuses one on a verb that does not - it tests
             "forcedMissRadius > 0f != CausesExplosion", an equality rather than a minimum, so a
             flat zero is a load error. The code that actually scatters a shot tests
             "ForcedMissRadius > 0.5f" instead. Anything in between therefore satisfies the
             check and never moves a grenade, which is what is wanted here: the bomb lands where
             the player put the ring, every time.

             Verb_ThrowFrostBomb.ThrownAt still carries the full scatter and will honour a real
             radius if this is raised, so this is one number to change and not a rewrite. -->
        <forcedMissRadius>0.4</forcedMissRadius>
        <warmupTime>0.8</warmupTime>
        <noiseRadius>4</noiseRadius>
        <!-- Never thrown on the pawn's own initiative. Pawn.TryGetAttackVerb skips a
             manual-cast verb while its owner is sitting in Wait_Combat, which is what a drafted
             pawn auto-acquiring targets is doing, so the bomb goes only where the player throws
             it. On a weapon that freezes allies as readily as enemies, a colonist who decides
             for themselves to lob one into their own firing line is not a feature. Explicit
             orders are unaffected. -->
        <onlyManualCast>true</onlyManualCast>
        <soundCast>ThrowGrenade</soundCast>
        <targetParams>
          <canTargetLocations>true</canTargetLocations>
          <canTargetPawns>true</canTargetPawns>
          <canTargetAnimals>true</canTargetAnimals>
          <canTargetMechs>true</canTargetMechs>
          <canTargetBuildings>false</canTargetBuildings>
          <canTargetSelf>false</canTargetSelf>
        </targetParams>
        <defaultProjectile>AG_FrostBombProjectile</defaultProjectile>
        <rangedFireRulepack>Combat_RangedFire_Thrown</rangedFireRulepack>
      </li>
    </verbs>
    <comps>
      <!-- A bomb in a fire cooks off, and freezes the room when it does. The same damage def
           carries the burst, so a rack of these going up in a warehouse blast reads exactly like
           a thrown one - which is the behaviour a player expects and the reason the freeze was
           moved onto the damage in the first place. -->
      <li Class="CompProperties_Explosive">
        <explosiveRadius>2.9</explosiveRadius>
        <explosiveDamageType>AG_Cryo</explosiveDamageType>
        <requiredDamageTypeToExplode>Flame</requiredDamageTypeToExplode>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
